      "analysis": {
        "summary": "**Ayurvedic Analysis for a 30‑year‑old male with 2‑day fever, cough, and body ache**\n\n---",
        "dosha_analysis": "- **Primary Imbalance**: **Pitta**  \n  *Fever (उष्णता) is the cardinal sign of Pitta vitiation. The heat of the body, the burning sensation in the chest, and the systemic body ache point to an excess of Pitta.*\n\n- **Sub‑Dosha Affected**: **Kapha**  \n  *The productive cough and the sense of heaviness in the throat suggest a secondary Kapha aggravation, which often accompanies Pitta‑dominated febrile illnesses.*\n\n- **Explanation**  \n  - **Pitta** governs temperature, metabolism, and transformation. A sudden rise in body temperature, along with a burning sensation, indicates that the digestive fire (Agni) is overactive and the metabolic processes are generating excess heat.  \n  - **Kapha** is the humoral component that becomes congested when Pitta is hot; it manifests as mucus, cough, and a feeling of heaviness.  \n  - The combination of fever, cough, and body ache is a classic “Pitta‑Kapha” pattern seen in acute respiratory infections.\n\n---",
        "dietary_recommendations": "### Foods to Favor (Pathya)\n| Food / Herb | Rationale |\n|-------------|-----------|\n| **Warm water (hot water)** | As per *Ashtanga Hridayam* (Hot water increases the power of digestion, cooks undigested food, good for throat diseases). It is cooling for Pitta and helps clear mucus. |\n| **Ginger (अद्रक)** | Pitta‑cooling, stimulates Agni, and has antitussive action. |\n| **Tulsi (तुलसी)** | Pitta‑balancing, antiviral, and supports lung function. |\n| **Turmeric (हल्दी)** | Pitta‑cooling, anti‑inflammatory, and boosts immunity. |\n| **Cooked rice or khichdi** | Easy to digest, supports Agni, and is neutral for Pitta. |\n| **Steamed or boiled vegetables (e.g., carrots, pumpkin)** | Light, cooling, and provide essential micronutrients. |\n| **Honey (मध)** | Pitta‑balancing, soothing for the throat, and has mild antimicrobial properties. |\n\n### Foods to Avoid (Apathya)\n| Food | Reason |\n|------|--------|\n| **Cold drinks & ice** | Increase Kapha, aggravate cough, and cool the stomach. |\n| **Heavy, fried, or oily foods** | Stagnate Agni, increase Kapha, and worsen body ache. |\n| **Dairy (milk, curd, cheese)** | Builds Kapha and can thicken mucus. |\n| **Spicy, pungent foods** | Exacerbate Pitta heat and irritate the throat. |\n| **Alcohol & caffeine** | Disrupt Agni and increase Pitta. |\n\n### Sample Meal Plan\n- **Breakfast**  \n  *Option 1*: Warm water + ½ tsp honey + 1 tsp grated ginger + ½ cup cooked oatmeal.  \n  *Option 2*: Warm milk (if tolerated) + 1 tsp turmeric + ½ cup boiled rice + a pinch of salt.\n\n- **Lunch**  \n  *Option 1*: Khichdi (rice + moong dal + carrots + cumin) + 1 cup warm ginger‑tulsi tea.  \n  *Option 2*: Steamed pumpkin + boiled egg + ½ cup warm water.\n\n- **Dinner**  \n  *Option 1*: Warm vegetable soup (carrot, pumpkin, coriander) + ½ cup boiled rice.  \n  *Option 2*: Steamed fish (if non‑vegetarian) + boiled spinach + ½ cup warm water.\n\n*All meals should be taken warm, not hot, and in moderate portions.*\n\n---",
        "herbal_formulations": "### Primary Formulation\n| Item | Details |\n|------|---------|\n| **Name** | *Pitta‑Kapha Balancing Kashaya* (Pitta‑Kapha‑Shamaka Kashaya) |\n| **Ingredients** | Ginger (अद्रक) 1 cm, Tulsi (तुलसी) 1 cm, Turmeric (हल्दी) ½ cm, Licorice (गिलोय) ½ cm, Honey (मध) 1 tsp, Warm water 1 L |\n| **Dosage** | 1 tsp (≈5 ml) in 100 ml warm water |\n| **Timing** | 2× daily – after breakfast and after dinner |\n\n### Supportive Formulations\n| Formulation | Key Ingredients | Use |\n|-------------|-----------------|-----|\n| **Amalaki Kashaya** | Amla (आंवला) 1 cm, Ginger ½ cm, Honey 1 tsp | 1 tsp 3× daily – boosts immunity |\n| **Vidanga Kashaya** | Vidanga (विधान्ग) ½ cm, Ginger ½ cm, Honey 1 tsp | 1 tsp 2× daily – antitussive & antimicrobial |\n\n> **Note**: If the cough becomes dry or the fever spikes, increase the dosage of the primary kashaya to 2 tsp, but do not exceed 3 tsp per day.\n\n---",
